  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 246 AM EST Sun Nov 8 2026\n\n- Mainly dry and mild through Tuesday\n\nNow that the upper low late last week has moved out of the area, we \nare back to our dry and mild El Nino fall for a few days. More \nspecifically, the northern branch of the upper jet has lifted north \nagain, and will mostly be a non-issue through Tuesday.\n\nWe are just close enough the the upper jet that a fairly strong \nupper wave embedded in it will try to just clip our northern \ncounties this afternoon, and bring a weak front through all of the \narea. A sprinkle or light shower can not be ruled out up north, but \nthe moisture over the area is quite dry to start with, with no \nsignificant moisture advection expected. We are likely to see some \nclouds develop along the front with some help from the lake this \nmorning. Additional clouds will stream in behind the wave tonight \nfrom the north, and some lake clouds are likely too. This cloud \ncover will be too shallow to do much more than the clouds.\n\nA ridge moves by on Monday which will eventually clear us out once \nagain. Then we look to see somewhat a repeat of today with a short \nwave moving by to our north, and a weak and dry cold front moving \nthrough on Tuesday. Even with these \"cold fronts\" moving through, \nthe air is not that much cooler with the deep cold pool staying \nnorth of the area, and the flow coming from the Pacific. Highs \nshould be in the 50s and 60s through Tuesday.\n\n- Much cooler and unsettled weather beginning Wed and beyond\n\nWe will see a change arrive on Wednesday as the upper air pattern \nover North America amplifies quite a bit. This is being driven by \nvery strong northern and southern jets over the Eastern Pacific. The \nmost notable feature is nearly a 500 dam 500 mb low over the \nnorthern Pacific. The downstream effects will be a strong ridge over \nthe Rockies, allowing strong long wave troughing developing over \nmuch of the Eastern U.S..\n\nWe will see a southern stream system approach the area on Wednesday \nfrom the west, with sufficient moisture advection ahead of it to \nbring decent rain chances to the area. This low establishes the \ntrough over the area, and then a strong northern stream trough dives \nsouth over the area with the establishment of the upper ridge to our \nwest. This phasing of the streams will help to develop a large upper \ntrough over much of the Eastern part of the country. This will allow \nfor a few more instability showers for our area, while the main \nenergy transfers to the East Coast.\n\nLake effect looks to get going also with the area firmly under the \nupper cold pool and cyclonic flow from Thursday and beyond. The \ngeneral consensus is that the flow will be mainly from N to S, \nfavoring areas over the lake and maybe the immediate lakeshore. \nDelta t's will be in the mid 20s C with 850 mb temps expected to \ndrop to around -6 to -7C. This degree of cold air, if it occurs, \nwould be cold enough for some of the showers to be snow showers away \nfrom the warmer lake. Not a lot of rain/snow chances will be present \ninland, but hit or miss showers will be present for much of next \nweekend.",
